#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""squidwho - which `http_access` line allows or denies a request, and
which `acl` lines decided it.

A static model of Squid's documented access rules
(DESIGN-squidwho.md, BUILD APPROVED 2026-11-09). Semantics pinned to
the Squid configuration manual (5.10 / 6.9 / 7.0), the Squid wiki
SquidFaq/SquidAcl, *Squid: The Definitive Guide* SS6.2, and the
squid-users 2012-06-20 note on http_access evaluation. Claims are
tagged [documented], [docs-inferred], or [hypothesized]; no Squid
binary exists on this box, so no claim here is [observed] and there
is no live-agreement test to skip-guard against: `squid -k parse`
validates syntax but dumps no access decisions, so hermetic fixture
trees are the only oracle.

Pinned semantics (SQ01-SQ24 in the design doc):
- SQ01 first match wins; AND within a rule, OR across rules; `!`
  negates one ACL element; a rule with no ACL elements matches
  unconditionally. Each directive type has its own rule list.
- SQ02 ACLs must be defined before use (single pass); same name +
  same type merges (values OR); same name + different type is fatal
  for rules using it; `all` is pre-defined as `src all` iff the
  file never defines it.
- SQ03 evaluable ACL types: src, dst, dstdomain, dstdom_regex,
  url_regex, urlpath_regex, port, myport/localport, localip/myip,
  method, proto, time. Everything else is SQ12.
- SQ04 value OR-logic inside one ACL (inline, merged, file-expanded).
- SQ05 http_access is request-only; no reply-side poisoning.
- SQ06 no-match default reverses the last rule; no rules at all
  means deny. A config ending in `http_access deny ...` with no
  trailing `deny all` silently allows everything unmatched.
- SQ07 URL composition incl. CONNECT authority-form (host:port,
  no path, no scheme); urlpath_regex tests the empty string for
  CONNECT. The composed URL is shown in the output.
- SQ08 dstdomain: exact host vs `.domain` (whole domain, incl. the
  bare domain), case-insensitive, one trailing dot stripped; the
  splay-tree overlap quirk is flagged and the union is evaluated;
  IP URLs need a reverse lookup (UNKNOWN) unless `-n` (FALSE), and
  a `none` pattern against an IP URL is UNKNOWN.
- SQ10 three-valued (Kleene) rules: FALSE dominates, then UNKNOWN,
  then TRUE; `!` keeps UNKNOWN unknown. A rule matches iff its AND
  is TRUE; an UNKNOWN rule before any TRUE rule is a blocker.
- SQ11 time ACLs: day abbreviations S M T W H F A, h1:m1 < h2:m2
  (reversed is a parse error), specs OR; `--at` is the reproducible
  form, otherwise local now with the [time-is-now-local] banner.
- SQ12 unmodeled dynamism is bannered and UNKNOWN-poisoning, never
  approximated.
- SQ13 the walk: first TRUE rule wins, else the SQ06 default.
- SQ15 regex is an approximation: POSIX classes translated to
  Python `re` where they coincide; untranslatable patterns are
  UNKNOWN; regex-decided verdicts carry [regex-approx]. `-i`/`+i`
  flags are per-pattern, tracked in order.
- SQ16-SQ24 structural findings, coverage on every run, inventory
  modes, the http_access-only banner, and the exact verdict
  vocabulary ALLOW / DENY / UNKNOWN.

The tool never runs squid, never opens a socket, never performs DNS,
never writes, and never reads the process environment, and opens
config, include, and ACL value files read-only.

Exit codes: 0 verdict ALLOW rendered (by rule or by SQ06 reversal,
labeled), or an inventory mode with >=1 item; 1 verdict DENY or
UNKNOWN, or an inventory mode with nothing listed; 2 usage error,
missing config, include depth exceeded / missing include file (SQ19),
or a --url without a scheme.
"""

